response:
 To solve the inequality \(\dfrac{x+1}{x+2}>\dfrac{3x+4}{2x+9}\), we begin by combining the fractions:
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{x+1}{x+2}-\dfrac{3x+4}{2x+9} &> 0 \\
&\Rightarrow \dfrac{(x+1)(2x+9)-(3x+4)(x+2)}{(x+2)(2x+9)}>0 \text{.}
\end{align*}
From here, we can cross-multiply and simplify:
\begin{align*}
2x^2+11x+9-3x^2-10x-8 &> 0 \\
-x^2+x+1 &> 0 \\
x^2-x-1 &< 0.
\end{align*}
When the roots of the quadratic are \(x_1\) and \(x_2\) with \(x_1<x_2\), the inequality \(x^2-x-1<0\) holds when \(x_1<x<x_2\). Using the quadratic formula, the roots are:
\begin{align*}
x_1 & =\frac{-(-1)-\sqrt{5}}{2} \text{ (neglect)} \\
& \textstyle =\frac{1-\sqrt{5}}{2} \\
x_2 & = \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2}.
\end{align*}
Thus, the solution to the inequality is \(x \in \left(\frac{1-\sqrt{5}}{2}, \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2}\right)\).

<answer> The solution to the inequality is \(x \in \left(\frac{1-\sqrt{5}}{2}, \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2}\right)\).

