 We know there are 13 cards of clubs. So, $\frac{13}{52}$. There 4 aces, so $\frac{4}{51}$. This is not multiplication because there can't be a 4 of clubs and 4 of diamonds to be the same (they are disjoint events). This is probability. </think> There $\frac{13\times 4}{52}$ as the following:
$
\begin{align}
&\quad\ \ \text{Possibility of all diamonds with aces in the second draw} \\
\\
\diamondsuit \,1^{\text{st}}&\text{ of club} \times \,A \,2^{\text{nd}}
+\diamondsuit \,1^{1 \text{st}} \text{ of spades} \times \,A \,2^{\text{nd}}
\diamondsuit \,1^{\text{st}} \text{ of hearts} \times \,A \,2^{\text{nd}}
\diamondsuit \,1^{1 \text{st}} \text{ of diamonds} \times \,A \,2^{\text{nd}}
\\
=&\ \dfrac{13}{52}\times \,\dfrac{4}{51}
+\dfrac{13}{52}\times \frac{4}{51} \,+\,\dfrac{13}{52} \,+\,\dfrac{13}{52}
\\[5pt]&=\,\dfrac{13}{52}\times \,\dfrac{4}{51} \times 4\ +\ \dfrac{13}{52}\times 4
\\[5pt]&= \dfrac{4}{51}
\\[10pt]
\end{align}
$
